.TH BABYSIT 1 "2026-11-15" "babysit" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
babysit \- run a command detached, log it, and return early if it stalls
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B babysit start
.RI [ options ]
.RB [ \-\- ]
.I command
.RI [ args ...]
.br
.B babysit watch
.I child_id
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B babysit check
.I child_id
.br
.B babysit info
.I child_id
.RB [ \-\-pid " | " \-\-log " | " \-\-exit\-code ]
.br
.B babysit signal
.I signal child_id
.br
.B babysit cat
.RB [ stdout ]
.RB [ stderr ]
.I child_id
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B babysit start
runs
.I command
in the background under a babysitter process, writes its output to a log
file and echoes it on stderr.
On stdout it prints one line and nothing else: the
.I identifier
of this run (the
.I child_id
the other commands take), such as
.BR 20261116/06/15/k3f9 ,
which is all a caller needs to find the log and the command again:
.PP
.RS
bbid=$(babysit start \-\-expect 'Listening on' \-\- ./server)
.RE
.PP
By default it waits for the command and returns its exit status.
With the options below it returns earlier: when an expected line appears,
when the command stops producing output, or after a fixed time.
.PP
Returning early never stops the command.
The command keeps running and its output keeps going to the log, so the
caller can come back later to read the log, wait longer, or kill it.
.PP
Three processes are involved:
.TP
.B parent
The
.B babysit
that was invoked.
It prints the identifier, waits for the babysitter to say why it should
return and turns that into an exit status.
.TP
.B babysitter
A detached process in its own session.
It reads the command's stdout and stderr, writes the log, echoes the log to
the caller's stderr, and runs the timers.
When the parent should return, it closes its copy of the caller's stderr and
carries on logging.
.TP
.B child
The command.
It gets
.I /dev/null
as stdin, pipes to the babysitter as stdout and stderr, and its own process
group.
It never holds the caller's terminal or pipes, so a caller that waits for
end-of-file on
.BR babysit 's
output is not kept waiting by it.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B start
Run a command as a new instance and print its identifier.
Everything above describes it.
.TP
.B watch
Attach to an instance that was started earlier and return as
.B start
would have: on an expected line, on silence, after a time limit, or with the
command's exit status when it exits.
With no options it simply waits for the command and returns its status; on
an instance that has already finished it returns at once.
The babysitter stopped reporting to anyone when
.B start
returned, so
.B watch
reads the log itself, from the beginning: a line printed before it attached
still satisfies
.BR \-\-expect .
Lines that appear while it watches are echoed on stderr.
.B \-\-idle
counts from the moment it attached.
Like
.BR start ,
it leaves the command running when it returns, unless
.B \-\-onfail
says otherwise.
.TP
.B check
Print
.B running
and exit 0 if the instance's command is running.
Otherwise exit 1, printing
.BI exited " N"
with its exit status, or
.B lost
if the babysitter died without recording one.
.TP
.B info
Print where the instance's log is, its state and, while the command is
running, its pid; or only the one thing asked for with
.BR \-\-pid ,
.B \-\-log
or
.BR \-\-exit\-code .
.TP
.B signal
Have the instance's babysitter send
.I signal
to the command's process group.
.I signal
is a name, with or without
.BR SIG ,
in either case
.RB ( INT ,
.BR sigterm ),
or a number.
The babysitter is the command's parent, so the signal cannot reach some
other process that has since been given the same pid; that is what makes
this safer than looking the pid up and calling
.BR kill (1).
Exits 1 if the command is not running.
.TP
.B cat
Print what the command has written so far, as the command wrote it: without
the
.B "OUT: "
and
.B "ERR: "
labels that the log puts before each line.
.B "cat stdout"
prints only what went to its stdout,
.B "cat stderr"
only what went to its stderr; with neither, or both, everything is printed,
in the order it is in the log.
The command line, the lines about the process and
.BR babysit 's
own notes are left out.
It works on a command that is still running, printing what there is so far,
and on one that has finished.
.IP
The output is the log's, so it is line by line as the log has it: a line
longer than 4096 bytes comes out in pieces, and output that stopped without
a newline for a second has one after it.
.SH OPTIONS
Options of
.B start
and
.BR watch :
.TP
.BI "\-e, \-\-expect " REGEX
Return 0 as soon as an output line matches the POSIX extended regular
expression
.IR REGEX .
The line is matched without its
.B OUT:
or
.B ERR:
prefix; lines from both streams are checked.
.TP
.B \-F, \-\-fixed
Treat the
.B \-\-expect
pattern as a plain substring.
.TP
.BI "\-i, \-\-idle " SECS
Return 125 if the command writes nothing to stdout or stderr for
.IR SECS .
Every byte restarts the timer.
.TP
.BI "\-t, \-\-timeout " SECS
Return 124 once
.I SECS
have passed in total.
.PP
Options of
.BR start :
.TP
.B \-d, \-\-detach
Return 0 as soon as the command has started.
.TP
.BI "\-c, \-\-config " FILE
Configuration file; see
.BR babysit.conf (5).
.B babysit
takes one setting from it,
.BR timezone .
Default:
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/babysit/babysit.conf ,
or
.IR $HOME/.config/babysit/babysit.conf .
A missing default file is not an error.
.PP
Options of
.BR watch :
.TP
.BI "\-\-onfail " SIGNAL
When returning 124 or 125, first have
.I SIGNAL
sent to the command, as
.B "babysit signal"
would.
.PP
Options of
.BR info :
.TP
.B \-\-pid
Print the pid of the command.
Exit 1, printing nothing, unless the command is running.
.TP
.B \-\-log
Print the path of the log.
.TP
.B \-\-exit\-code
Print the command's exit status.
Exit 1, printing nothing, until the command has exited.
.PP
Options of every command:
.TP
.BI "\-l, \-\-log\-dir " DIR
Base log directory.
The default is
.IR $HOME/.local/var/log/babysit .
The commands that take an identifier must be given the same directory as the
.B start
that printed it.
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
Show the version.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Show a summary of options.
.PP
For
.BR start ,
options must come before the command to run; the other commands take them
before or after the identifier.
Options that take a value accept it as the next argument, or after
.B =
in the long form.
Short options cannot be combined or have the value attached:
.B "\-d \-t 30"
and
.BR \-\-timeout=30 ,
not
.B \-dt30
or
.BR \-t30 .
.PP
.I SECS
may be fractional and may carry an
.BR s ,
.B m
or
.B h
suffix (0.5, 30s, 2m).
.SH INSTANCES AND LOG FILES
Every run of
.B babysit
is an
.I instance
with an identifier of its own:
.PP
.RS
.IR YYYYmmdd / HH / MM / salt
.RE
.PP
the minute it started in and four random characters.
The identifier is also where the instance lives.
Its log is
.PP
.RS
.IR LOGDIR / YYYYmmdd / HH / MM / salt /log
.RE
.PP
so an identifier can be read, and its log opened, without asking
.B babysit
anything.
Instances started close together share most of their identifier and differ
at the end, where it is easy to see.
.PP
No two instances get the same identifier.
The last directory is created at the moment the identifier is chosen, which
fails if it already exists, and another salt is then tried.
The salt is made of lower-case letters and digits, without
.BR 0 ,
.BR o ,
.B 1
and
.BR l ,
which are too easily taken for one another.
.PP
The date and time are local time, or UTC if
.BR babysit.conf (5)
says
.BR "timezone = utc" .
Use UTC where
.B babysit
is driven by automation: local time repeats an hour every autumn where
clocks are set back, with the consequence described under
.BR "WHEN THE CLOCK GOES BACKWARDS" .
.PP
Nothing in an identifier or a log path is a process id.
A pid says which process something is only for as long as that process is
alive; afterwards the number is given to something else.
.PP
The log contains, in order:
.IP \(bu 2
the command line;
.IP \(bu 2
.BI "Process created with pid " N ;
.IP \(bu 2
every line of output, prefixed with
.B "OUT: "
(stdout) or
.B "ERR: "
(stderr);
.IP \(bu 2
.BI "Process exited with code " N
or
.BI "Process killed by signal " N
once the command is gone.
.PP
If the log cannot be written while the command is running, because the disk
has filled up or a file size limit was reached, the command is left running
and its output is still echoed.
.B babysit
prints one warning on stderr, and if writing works again later the log
gets a line saying how many bytes are missing at that point.
A log that ends without a
.B "Process exited"
or
.B "Process killed"
line is not proof that the command is still running.
.PP
Output that does not end in a newline, such as a prompt, is logged as a line
of its own after one second, or at once when an idle stall is reported.
Lines longer than 4096 bytes are split.
.PP
Two more files can appear beside the log.
.I exit_code
is written when the command exits and holds its exit status as one number,
as a shell would report it: 128 plus the signal number if it was killed by
a signal, and 126 or 127 if it could not be executed at all.
It is there from the moment the status is known, so
.PP
.RS
cat $LOGDIR/$bbid/exit_code
.RE
.PP
is a complete answer to "how did it end", and its absence means that it has
not ended, or that nobody was left to say.
.I ctl
is a FIFO through which
.B "babysit signal"
reaches the babysitter; it exists only while the command is running.
.PP
Finished logs do not stay where they are for ever:
.BR babysitd (8)
packs past days into
.IR LOGDIR /archive
and deletes old ones.
It collects the logs of commands that have finished, once they are past the
window set in
.BR babysit.conf (5)
.RB ( archive_after_days ,
or sooner on a full disk).
The log of a command that is still running stays where it is, under its
identifier, however long ago it was started, and is therefore missing from
the archive made for its day if it had not finished by then; it is
collected later, into a further archive for the same day.
Without
.BR babysitd (8)
nothing is packed or deleted, and logs simply accumulate.
.SH USING THE IDENTIFIER
The identifier is what to keep.
Everything else is asked for with it, when it is needed.
.PP
.RS
.nf
# start always prints an identifier once there is an instance
bbid=$(babysit start \-\-expect 'Listening on' \-\-timeout 30s \-\- ./server)
rc=$?
case $rc in
  0)   echo "server is up" ;;
  124) echo "no sign of life after 30s, stopping it" >&2
       babysit signal TERM "$bbid"; exit $rc ;;
  *)   echo "server died during start\-up (status $rc)" >&2; exit $rc ;;
esac

# later: come back if it goes quiet for 30 seconds, and interrupt it then
babysit watch "$bbid" \-\-idle 30s \-\-onfail INT \-\-expect "$done" || {
    echo "no '$done' and no output for 30s"
}

# is it still there?
babysit check "$bbid" || echo "server is dead"
.fi
.RE
.PP
A pid is never part of this.
.B "babysit info \-\-pid"
does print the command's pid, but only while the command is running.
The babysitter holds a lock for exactly as long as it has not collected the
command's exit status, and until then the pid cannot be given to any other
process; once the lock is gone
.B babysit
prints nothing and exits 1, because the number may by then belong to
something else.
A pid is therefore right at the moment it is printed.
It can still go stale between that moment and its use, which is why
.B "babysit signal"
exists and why the identifier, not the pid, is the thing to store.
.PP
Give the same
.B \-\-log\-dir
as
.B start
had, if it had one.
If
.BR babysitd (8)
has since packed or expired the instance, these commands say so and exit 2.
.SH WHEN THE CLOCK GOES BACKWARDS
Identifiers are dated, and they are only worth reading if the dates are
true.
If the log directory holds records dated later than now, the clock has been
set back since they were filed (or the
.B timezone
setting has changed, or local time has just repeated an hour).
New records would be filed in among older ones that claim to be newer.
.PP
.B babysit
does not let that happen.
It starts nothing, prints nothing on stdout, says on stderr which record is
in the way, and exits with status
.BR 75 ,
which means: nothing is wrong with the command, try again later.
It does not move or pack anything itself.
.PP
The next step belongs to
.BR babysitd (8),
which packs later-dated records that are finished: days into the archive,
later hours and minutes of today in place.
If the service is running,
.B babysit
has already woken it by the time it returns, and a retry a second or two
later succeeds.
If it is not, run
.PP
.RS
babysitd \-\-once
.RE
.PP
and try again.
A caller driving
.B babysit
automatically should treat 75 as it would
.BR EAGAIN :
wait briefly, retry, and give up after a few attempts.
.PP
A retry always gets through in the end.
Only finished records count as being in the way, and those are exactly the
ones
.BR babysitd (8)
can pack.
An instance dated later than now that is still being written is not held
against a new one: nobody could pack it, and refusing on its account would
mean refusing for as long as its command cares to run.
When it does finish, it becomes a finished later-dated record like any
other, and the next
.B "babysit start"
may get one more 75 while the service packs it.
The one thing a retry can still run into is a record finished in the last
two seconds, which
.B babysitd
does not touch yet; the retry after that succeeds.
.PP
With
.B "timezone = utc"
none of this is triggered by daylight saving time.
For the same reason one log directory should be used with one setting, and
by machines that agree on the time.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Of
.B start
and
.BR watch :
.TP
.I N
The command exited with status
.I N
(128 plus the signal number if it was killed by a signal).
.TP
.B 0
.B \-\-expect
matched, or
.B \-\-detach
was given.
.TP
.B 1
.B \-\-expect
was given but the command exited with status 0 without a matching line.
.TP
.B 124
.B \-\-timeout
expired; the command is still running.
.TP
.B 125
Nothing was written for
.B \-\-idle
seconds; the command is still running.
.TP
.B 126, 127
The command could not be executed, or was not found.
No log is left behind.
.TP
.B 75
The log directory holds records dated later than now.
Nothing was started; try again once
.BR babysitd (8)
has packed them.
See
.BR "WHEN THE CLOCK GOES BACKWARDS" .
.TP
.B 2
Usage or internal error.
Nothing was started, with one exception: if
.B babysit
loses contact with the babysitter after the command has started, it says so
on stderr and returns 2 although the command may still be running (see
.BR CAVEATS ).
.PP
.B check
exits 0 if the command is running and 1 if it is not.
.B info
exits 0, or 1 if what was asked for does not exist at the moment
.RB ( \-\-pid
of a command that is not running,
.B \-\-exit\-code
of one that is).
.B signal
exits 0 once the babysitter has been asked, and 1 if the command is not
running.
.B cat
exits 0.
All of them exit 2 if there is no such instance.
.PP
The reason for returning is also printed on stderr.
A command that itself exits with one of the special statuses cannot be told
apart by the status of
.B start
or
.B watch
alone;
.B "babysit check"
settles it.
.SH NOTES
The identifier is printed on stdout once the command has started, and
stdout is then finished with: a caller capturing it is not kept waiting.
Nothing is printed there if nothing was started.
.PP
The command's output is echoed to the caller's stderr for as long as
.B babysit
has not returned.
If the caller reads it slowly, or stops reading without closing it, the
babysitter waits, and once the pipes in between are full the command waits
too, as it would in any pipeline.
.B \-\-timeout
still expires on time while this is going on, and
.B babysit
then returns and the command carries on, logging only.
Time spent waiting for the caller is not the command's silence and does not
count towards
.BR \-\-idle .
.PP
Killing or interrupting
.B babysit
does not stop the command.
The babysitter notices that its parent is gone, releases the caller's
stderr, and keeps logging.
.PP
If the command exits while its own children still hold its stdout or stderr
open, the exit is reported and logged at once, and their later output is
still appended to the log.
.SH CAVEATS
.B babysit
reports what it can observe from outside: bytes arriving on two pipes, a
line that matches, the clock, an exit status.
It does not know whether the command is healthy, making progress or stuck,
and it never stops the command.
Each of its answers means exactly what is said below and no more; what to do
about it is for the caller to decide.
.SS "\-\-idle means no output, not hung"
A command that is working hard without printing anything is reported as
stalled, and one that is stuck in a loop printing the same line is not.
Status 125 is a reason to go and look, not a verdict.
.PP
Output buffering makes this worse than it sounds.
Most programs flush each line when stdout is a terminal but collect several
kilobytes before writing when it is a pipe, as it is under
.BR babysit .
A program that visibly prints a line a second in a terminal can therefore be
silent for minutes here and deliver everything at once, or only when it
exits.
Where that matters, turn the buffering off in the command:
.B "stdbuf \-oL"
.I command
for programs using C stdio,
.B "python \-u"
or
.BR PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1 ,
and the equivalent for other runtimes.
The same delay applies to
.BR \-\-expect .
.SS "\-\-expect means a line matched, not ready"
The pattern is tried against each line as it is written to the log, and a
match says only that such a line was printed.
A logged line is at most 4096 bytes, and output that stops without a newline
for a second is logged as a line of its own, so a pattern cannot match
across either kind of break.
Text after a NUL byte in a line is not seen.
A prompt that does not end in a newline is matched only once it has been
logged, about a second after it was printed.
.SS "\-\-detach means started, not running"
Status 0 from
.B \-\-detach
says that the command was executed.
A command that fails a moment later, on a bad argument for instance, has
still been started; its log says how it ended.
.SS "The exit status is that of the command itself"
.B babysit
waits for the one process it started.
A command that puts itself in the background, as many daemons do, exits at
once with status 0, and that is what is reported, or status 1 if
.B \-\-expect
had not matched by then.
Whatever it left running keeps its log as long as it holds the command's
stdout or stderr open, but
.B babysit
knows nothing about how it fares.
Run such programs in the foreground where they have an option for it.
.SS "The babysitter can die"
The babysitter is an ordinary process.
If it is killed, by the out-of-memory killer for instance, logging stops, the
log is left without its final
.B "Process exited"
or
.B "Process killed"
line, and the command, which is now writing to pipes nobody reads, is killed
by SIGPIPE the next time it prints something, unless it ignores that signal.
A
.B babysit
that was still waiting reports that it lost contact and returns 2.
No
.I exit_code
is ever written for such an instance, and
.B "babysit check"
reports it as
.BR lost .
.SS "Detached from the terminal, not from everything"
The babysitter and the command leave the caller's session and terminal, so
closing the terminal or killing the caller does not touch them.
They stay in the caller's control group, though.
Whatever tears that down takes them with it: systemd at logout when
.B KillUserProcesses
is set, a service manager stopping the unit that
.B babysit
was called from, a CI runner or container ending the job.
To outlive those, start
.B babysit
from something that puts it in a group of its own, such as
.BR "systemd\-run \-\-user \-\-scope" .
.SS "The command has no terminal"
Its stdin is
.IR /dev/null ,
its stdout and stderr are pipes, and it has no controlling terminal.
Programs notice: many drop colours and progress bars, and anything that
needs to ask a question or read a password fails or gives up.
.B babysit
is for commands that run unattended.
.PP
Stdout and stderr arrive on separate pipes.
Each stream's lines are logged in order, but when both are written at
nearly the same moment, the order of an
.B OUT:
line relative to an
.B ERR:
line is not certain.
.SS "Logs can hold secrets"
A log starts with the full command line, arguments included, and records
everything the command prints.
The log directory and the logs are created with the caller's umask, which
on many systems leaves them readable by other users.
Keep passwords and tokens out of arguments, and set a stricter umask or
restrict
.I LOGDIR
if what is logged should stay private.
.SH EXAMPLES
Start a server and return once it is listening:
.PP
.RS
babysit start \-\-expect 'Listening on' \-\-timeout 30 \-\- ./server \-\-port 8080
.RE
.PP
Run a build, but come back if it goes quiet for two minutes:
.PP
.RS
babysit start \-\-idle 2m \-\- make \-j8
.RE
.SH \&
We are aware that
.B babysitter
and
.B baby
is a bad metaphor.
We are aware that the babysitter is the parent process, and we are aware
that our docs refer to 'babysitter' and 'child' even though it could have
more internally consistent to use 'babysitter' and 'baby'.
We like it more the way it is.
We will
.I never
change the documentation or example code.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR babysitd (8),
.BR babysit.conf (5),
.BR timeout (1)
